[00:35.73]Conversation One
[00:37.68]W: Good afternoon, Mr.Green.I'm planning to go abroad for study.
[00:42.70]Do you think I should get some health insurance?
[00:45.29]M: I think so.
[00:46.88]One unexpected medical emergency can drain your financial resources,leaving you no money to pay for school.
[00:56.09]As a result,you'll have no choice but to return to your own country.
[01:00.75]W: How can I get health insurance?
[01:03.31]M: You really have two basic choices for health insurance: obtain international insurance in your home country before you depart or student health insurance once you arrive.
[01:16.30]W: Then how to get international insurance?
[01:19.60]M: As for international insurance, you'll have to pay the medical costs in full on your own.
[01:25.77]If you have sufficient funds, then this won't be a problem.
[01:29.27]W: Oh, that seems not suitable for me.
[01:32.17]What about the second option?
[01:34.25]M: As for the second option,you'll probably just have to pay a certain percentage,and the company will pay their part.
[01:42.35]W: That sounds good.
[01:43.79]Then what should we note when signing the insurance agreement?
[01:48.29]M: Well, be sure to clearly understand the terms of the agreement you sign with the insurance company.
[01:55.28]There are often a number of limitations and restrictions with the policy,and fully understanding them will reduce the number of misunderstandings that could arise.
[02:04.71]W: Oh, I see.
[02:06.06]M: It is wise of you to consider health insurance before going abroad to study.
[02:11.18]However,many students only think about insurance when it is too late as they sit in the hospital's emergency room.
[02:18.76]So get insurance to help you pay for your medical expenses;otherwise, you might not have money for tomorrow's school tuition.
[02:26.29]W: Well, I think I've got all the information I need.
[02:29.56]Thank you so much, Mr.Green.
[02:31.51]M: You're welcome.
[02:33.69]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:38.29]1. What is the possible result of having no health insurance when one studies abroad?
[02:59.21]2. What kind of insurance does the woman most probably take?
[03:16.87]3.What should one pay attention to when signing an insurance contract?
[03:35.88]4. What do we learn from the end of the conversation?
[03:52.86]Conversation Two
[03:54.65]M: Why, Mary Smith.
[03:57.00]I haven't seen you for ages.
[03:58.97]How have you been?
[04:00.03]W: John! John Brown!
[04:02.77]It has been a long time,hasn't it?
[04:05.33]It must be at least a year.
[04:07.35]M: No, we talked at the Johnson's Christmas party last December.
[04:11.45]Don't you remember?
[04:12.55]W: That's right.
[04:14.33]Well, how are you?
[04:15.89]Still working for the Jones and French Company?
[04:18.88]M: No, I changed the job about three months ago.
[04:22.20]I'm with the National Bank now.
[04:23.95]How about you?
[04:24.93]W: I'm still teaching at the university.
[04:27.53]But I switched from the German department to the Spanish department.
[04:31.61]M: Don't tell me you speak Spanish, too.
[04:34.38]W: Sure. I grew up in Spain, you know.
[04:37.12]German was what I studied in college, but my Spanish is much better.
[04:42.06]M: All I've ever managed to learn is a little Italian.
[04:45.27]I can get a pizza in Rome,but not much more.
[04:48.80]W: Well, how is your family?
[04:51.00]Are all the children in school now?
[04:53.09]M: No, Billy is still at home.
[04:55.52]Tom is in the third grade and Jane's in the second.
[04:58.87]Billy could have gone to kindergarten,but we didn't want him to start school too early.
[05:04.05]So we're keeping him out until next year.
[05:06.49]W: Our two children haven't started school yet either.
[05:09.66]But my daughter Sue will go in the fall.
[05:12.39]Oh, my husband just came in.
[05:14.47]I want to go and ask him something.
[05:16.67]I'll talk to you again later, John.
[05:18.50]M: It was nice seeing you again, Mary.
[05:20.72]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
