[07:09.40]Passage One
[07:10.89]Tonight,I'll be specifically talking about word-of-mouth advertising.
[07:16.34]As we all know,billions of dollars are spent on advertising every year.
[07:21.71]Well, do you prefer to buy products recommended by an advertiser or your friends?
[07:28.31]Personally, I prefer the latter,because I trust my friends.
[07:32.86]So, word-of-mouth advertising happens when a person tells another person about the good experience with a product or service.
[07:41.96]That second person then tells another friend,family member, or colleague.
[07:47.72]By contrast,traditional advertisers talk about how good their products are.
[07:53.29]Usually, they say things like,"Studies show that our products are the best"or "Everyone loves our products" but it can sound insincere or unconvincing.
[08:04.57]And some statistics also prove that word-of-mouth advertising is more reliable and effective.
[08:11.52]According to a new study from NOP World,93% of people believe word-of-mouth is the most reliable way to find out information about products and services,70% of people are affected by it,37% will buy it or try it,24% will consider it and merely 9% will avoid it.
[08:33.84]So, it's obvious that word-of-mouth advertising has many advantages like high credibility, low costs,great reach and high efficiency.
[08:44.10]Do you agree with me?
[08:45.89]And the main reasons for the wide popularity of word-of-mouth advertising are also apparent.
[08:52.32]Socializing and networking have now made people closer and closer.
[08:57.84]So relatives and friends are simply accessible over the net.
[09:03.86]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[09:09.17]9. What does the speaker say about purchasing products?
[09:27.38]10.What do we learn from the new study of NOP World?
[09:46.87]11.What is the main reason for the wide popularity of word-of-mouth advertising?
[10:06.42]Passage Two
[10:08.29]Movies often depict the elderly as ill-tempered.
[10:11.99]In fact, most elder people have fewer negative feelings,like anger, than the young.
[10:18.29]Recent research into how emotions change with age may be able to help people lead healthier and longer lives and bring about new treatments for depression in the elderly.
[10:30.11]Like people's bodies,emotions change over time.
[10:33.96]Older people for the most part have far fewer negative feelings,such as worry and stress,than younger people do, studies show.
[10:43.62]The elderly learn to free themselves from feelings of negativity and seem to focus more on present situations that bring pleasure,rather than on the future, researchers say.
[10:54.92]They also tend to process negative information less deeply than positive information.
[11:01.18]By contrast,positive feelings such as enjoyment and happiness change very little from the time when a person is in his youth until old age.
[11:11.09]"It seems to be essential for our emotional well-being to not look back in anger and to focus on the positive when we are older," Stefanie Brassen, a researcher at the University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf,said in an email.
[11:26.87]Older people who are depressed seem not to use the sort of emotional regulation typically seen in the elderly, researchers say.
[11:35.06]Depression affects some 6 million Americans over the age of 65,or about 15% of the total,and is believed to be a big reason why the elderly have the highest suicide rate, according to the nonprofit Geriatric Mental Health Foundation.
[11:52.09]In a potential new treatment approach,Dr. Brassen and his colleagues are developing a training program in which older people practice strategies to better adapt to life circumstances.
[12:05.82]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[12:11.51]12.How do movies depict the old?
[12:29.03]13. According to the researchers,what do the old pay more attention to?
[12:49.46]14.What changes less over time as a person getting old?
[13:09.02]15.What is an important factor contributing to the highest suicide rate in the American elderly?
