[13:58.74]Recording One
[14:00.26]The revolution in communications that has already taken place is still not fully understood.
[14:06.78]One way of appreciating it is to do a kind of communications strip tease.
[14:12.01]I would like you to remove in your minds TV,then radio, then telephones,then the postal service,then the newspapers.
[14:21.87]In such a situation,we should feel deaf and blind.
[14:26.11]Well, we'll appear this way to our grandchildren.
[14:30.01]Don't forget that a generation has already grown up that never knew a world without TV.
[14:36.50]One communications revolution has taken place in our lifetime.
[14:40.91]The next revolution,perhaps the final one,will be the result of satellites and microelectronics, which will enable us to do literally anything we want to in the field of communications and information transfer！including, ultimately,not only sound and vision but all sense impressions.
[15:00.54]I am particularly interested in TV broadcasting from satellites directly into the home,bypassing today's ground stations！a proposal I first described 22 years ago.
[15:12.61]This will mean the abolition of all present geographical restrictions to TV;via satellites,any country can broadcast to any other.
[15:22.33]Direct-broadcast TV will be possible within five years and may be most important to undeveloped countries that have no ground stations,and now may never require any.
[15:34.22]I believe that communications satellites will certainly lead to a global telephone system and end long-distance calls！for all calls will be "local"!
[15:44.30]There will be the same flat rate everywhere.
[15:47.26]All these things are associated with information processing, and one-third of the GNP is now spent on this in one form or another data storage, TV, radio, books, and so forth.
[16:01.15]This ratio is increasing; our society is changing from a goods-producing society to an information-processing one.
[16:10.04]Another very important consequence will be a change in the patterns of transport, for a man and his work need no longer be in the same place！anybody who does any kind of mental work can live wherever he pleases.
[16:24.06]Beyond this, any kind of manipulative skill can also be transferred from one point to another.
[16:31.25]I can imagine a time when even a brain surgeon can live in one place and operate on patients all over the world, through remote-controlled artificial hands.
[16:41.95]OK. That's all for today's talk.
[16:44.87]Thank you for listening.
[16:47.62]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[16:53.47]16. What does the speaker say about communications revolution?
[17:13.68]17. What CANNOT be transferred in the next communications revolution?
[17:33.14]18. What does the speaker say about the change in the patterns of transport?
[17:52.58]Recording Two
[17:54.41]Good evening, everyone.
[17:55.96]Over the last few weeks we discussed a method for teaching debate to English learners.
[18:01.64]Debate is a valuable way to practice communicating.
[18:05.02]It can also bring long-lasting rewards outside the classroom,especially for people working with Western businesses.
[18:12.25]The main activity of debate is presenting one's opinion and supporting it with evidence,such as statistics or facts.
[18:20.06]It is a way of persuasive communication！of making someone believe what you are saying.
[18:25.58]Charles Lebeau helped create the "Discover Debate" method.
[18:29.49]He says debate is important to understand how people communicate in Western business.
[18:35.14]Successful debaters learn how to give their opinion,reasons and support.
[18:40.19]He adds that people use persuasive communication in meetings too.
[18:44.97]Three or four people may present ideas.
[18:47.73]Then, they attack or support each other's arguments.
[18:51.05]This way of approaching presenting opinions and responding to opinions is very important for English speakers.
[18:58.46]In Japan, people don't really do that.
[19:01.32]In English-style meetings,people do do that and Japanese participants need to do that in an English style meeting in order to be successful.
[19:10.69]Debate also strengthens critical thinking.
[19:14.10]In other words,it helps students learn to ask questions and try to understand someone's reasons and evidence.
[19:20.87]Mr.Lebeau points out that successful debaters learn to listen carefully to what other people are saying.
[19:27.25]Then, they look for the weak points in someone else's opinion or argument.
[19:31.77]He says debate teaches a systematic way of questioning.
[19:35.78]Successful debaters also learn to think from someone else's point of view.
[19:40.58]Mr.Lebeau says debate can help broaden the mind.
[19:44.19]There's an expression in English: Don't criticize another person before you have walked in their shoes.
[19:50.27]The wonderful thing about debate is that it puts us in another person's shoes.
[19:54.99]For example, students in a debate might argue the topic "soccer  is a better sport than baseball".
[20:02.21]A student may have to argue in favor of soccer,even if he or she does not really think soccer is better than baseball.
[20:09.97]But the student will have to explain why soccer is an interesting sport and present the viewpoint of soccer fans.
[20:17.06]The exercise will offer a way of thinking the student may not have normally had.
[20:22.20]OK, thank you very much for listening.
[20:26.23]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[20:32.53]19. According to the speaker,who can particularly benefit from debate?
[20:53.33]20. What do we learn about successful debaters from Charles Lebeau?
[21:12.36]21. What does the speaker say about debate?
[21:30.52]Recording Three
[21:32.83]A public opinion study says that most Americans would accept the use of gene-editing technology to create babies who are protected from some diseases. 
[21:43.60]But the same study finds that Americans do not support changing the genetic structure, or DNA, of children so that they are faster, taller or more intelligent. 
[21:56.00]The research was carried out by the Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research. 
[22:03.13]The opinion study suggests that many people are concerned about what gene-editing means for the future. 
[22:10.15]More than 1,000 people were questioned in the study. 
[22:14.26]The researchers said Americans value the medical promise of technology that could change the genetic qualities human parents pass on to their children. 
[22:24.28]But they worry whether it will be used in a moral way.
[22:28.02]Gene editing takes out a part of DNA to remove, replace or repair a gene. 
[22:35.49]Changes to adult cells only affect the person being treated. 
[22:40.41]But editing genes in eggs, sperm or embryos can change the resulting child in ways that can be passed to future generations. 
[22:50.21]Because of its important effects, international science guidelines say gene-editing should not yet be tested in human pregnancies. 
[22:59.72]Scientists say more laboratory research is needed to prove whether or not it is safe.  
[23:06.26]Dr. Robert Klitzman studies biological research rules. 
[23:10.48]He noted that, if fertility clinics start to edit the genes of embryos, there will be hard choices to make about what conditions can be changed.  
[23:21.04]What if scientists could identify genes involved with depression or autism or high body weight? 
[23:28.61]Would they be acceptable to edit? 
[23:30.89]"It's one thing to look at the extremes of fatal diseases versus cosmetic things, but in the middle are going to be these very different issues," Klitzman said.  
[23:42.10]More Americans oppose than approve of government support for testing gene-editing technology on human embryos. 
[23:50.36]About 25 percent of them have no opinion. 
[23:54.20]Jaron Keener, a Pittsburgh museum worker, said that he opposes the research because he fears it would not be used only to fight disease. 
[24:04.22]"I just don't have a lot of confidence people wouldn't use it for their own gain," he said. 
[24:10.07]The study suggests a lack of trust in the technology. 
[24:13.86]About one-third of Americans think gene editing will be used before it is fully tested. 
[24:20.20]Nearly 90 percent of the people in the study thought that the technology will be used for the wrong reasons.
[24:28.31]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[24:35.44]22. What does the public opinion study find?
[24:54.58]23. What are Americans concerned about the gene-editing technology? 
[25:14.01]24. What does the international science guidelines say about gene-editing? 
[25:34.55]25. What is the museum worker Jaron Keener¨s attitude towards gene-editing? 
